        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"flaubert-madame-bovary-235416d6f3edeafa\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Charles directly presents his continuing love for Emma as an established fact: “Tu sais bien que je t’aime.” Its immediate purpose is to reassure and persuade her to calm down and approach him, but it does not itself undertake a new relational commitment. Although prompted by tenderness, discouragement, and distress, the wording is a deliberate avowal rather than an expressive or reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The utterance invokes their existing marital relationship and has a conciliatory aim, but reassurance and relational appeal alone do not supply independent performative force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Charles directly addresses Emma in spoken dialogue after she rejects his request for a kiss.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Charles cannot comprehend Emma’s intense hostility and responds by reminding her of what he treats as mutually known—his love—while urging her to calm herself and come to him. The avowal is sincere within the presented scene but tragically ineffective: Emma is inwardly estranged from him, preoccupied with Rodolphe, and answers “Assez!” before fleeing. This dramatic irony affects reception and narrative meaning, not the core utterance’s predominantly truth-conditional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Tu sais bien que je t’aime!” (“You know very well that I love you!”)\",\"supports\":\"The knowledge construction presents Charles’s love as an already established, continuing state, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand, the narrator says Charles turns toward Emma “par tendresse subite et découragement,” asks her to kiss him, and then reacts with stupefaction to her rejection.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is affectively motivated and serves as reassurance or persuasion, but the organized conversational response does not positively indicate reflex-like expressive force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The avowal is embedded among directives: “Calme-toi! reprends-toi!...” and “viens!”\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding speech seeks reconciliation and compliance; those aims belong to the context and adjacent directives rather than turning the love avowal itself into an undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Emma replies “Assez!”, slams the door, and Charles collapses, weeping and unable to understand her behavior.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance fails as reassurance because of the spouses’ estrangement, while remaining an avowal of Charles’s emotional state.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as affirmative present-tense French “je t’aime” in direct dialogue in Gustave Flaubert’s Madame Bovary.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the identified core occurrence and its present-state avowal form.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Its conciliatory purpose, emotional delivery, and unsuccessful reception are adequately represented as context and do not require P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
